{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And if she has not been defiled, etc., and she is pure, etc.</b> He doubled the saying ״וטהורה״ (and she is pure), because he comes to say that besides the woman being acquitted by the waters and their not destroying her, she still has ״ונקתה ונזרעה זרע״ (she shall be cleared and shall retain seed). Therefore he stipulated <b>וטהורה</b> — meaning, she was not defiled by the act of lying with a man other than her husband, <b>and she is pure</b> from a reprehensible matter, which is the practice of adulterers that precedes intercourse — lust for another, and embracing and kissing and the like. Then it is that the waters will bestow good upon her with all that is written. But if she was not defiled yet was not purified from the branches of sin, although she will be spared the punishment, the benefits that are stated will not reach her." "And you will find that in the priest's adjuration he said <b>if you have gone astray, etc.</b> <b>and if you have been defiled, etc.</b>, and it is difficult: what is his intention in the statement ״כי שטית״ (if you have gone astray)? If it means ״כי נטמאת״ (if you have been defiled) — he should not have said ״וכי נטמאת״ with the added vav. Rather, he certainly intended the matter of things that precede defilement, which is lust for another and so forth, and he stipulated <b>וכי נטמאת</b> — only then is it that there would be punishment. And he stated the converse: if neither of the two categories was present, she will be acquitted from the evil and will succeed in a good thing, and the middle category is understood from the middle of the matter. And our Rabbis said (Sotah 18a) ״וטהורה היא״ — from another man; and according to our approach, 'another man' is included in the statement לא נטמאה (she has not been defiled), for as a general rule he stated both from this man and from another."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" 